{
  "id": "r-27",
  "level": 5,
  "title": "anti_join and semi_join: finding what's missing",
  "explanation": "Sometimes you don't want to combine tables. You want to **filter** one table by whether it has a match in another: which players bought something, and which didn't? **semi_join()** keeps rows that have a match, and **anti_join()** keeps rows that don't. Neither adds any columns.",
  "datasets": [
    "games",
    "studios",
    "players",
    "sales"
  ],
  "starter_code": "",
  "tasks": [
    {
      "teach": "**anti_join()** is the fastest way to find what's missing: customers who never ordered, products never sold, or IDs that don't match because of a typo.",
      "prompt": "Find the studios with no games in our table: result <- studios |> anti_join(games, by = \"studio_id\").",
      "starter_code": "",
      "check": {
        "type": "data_frame",
        "object": "result",
        "columns": [
          "studio_id",
          "studio_name",
          "country",
          "founded_year"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            11,
            "Valve",
            "USA",
            1996
          ],
          [
            12,
            "Ubisoft",
            "France",
            1986
          ]
        ],
        "ignore_order": false,
        "must_use": [
          "anti_join"
        ]
      },
      "hints": [
        "anti_join keeps rows of studios with NO match in games.",
        "result <- studios |> anti_join(games, by = \"studio_id\")"
      ],
      "answer": "result <- studios |> anti_join(games, by = \"studio_id\")"
    },
    {
      "teach": "Unlike inner_join, **semi_join()** never repeats a row: a player with 3 purchases still appears once.",
